Z||000014||2birds||11-09-2001||09:46 PM||||Yes, the VFW does sell poppies here.  Don't see many, but I think the VFW membership has really dropped over the years.  Thankfully, we haven't had a major (like WW II) shooting war in the last 56 years.  War is a necessary evil, since smarter heads don't always prevail initially, but hopefully we can avoid the tremendous losses suffered by all sides in a major war.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||BC-GT||11-09-2001||11:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by blazin':<BR><B><BR>The school I attend, we have a ceremony for the vet's, and last year, during the moment of silence, people were laughing and talking their heads off! All the while, there were like five veterans in attendence. I can't imagine the feeling of disrespect they must have felt</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's truely disgusting, I don't generally get pissed at much, but I'd probably start swinging at those f*ckers for something like that. Anyone here think they got a hard gig? try a couple of days in the trenches like they did in ww1. This is the ONE holiday I got lots of respect for.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||tgowens||11-09-2001||11:50 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 2birds:<BR><B> Thankfully, we haven't had a major (like WW II) shooting war in the last 56 years.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, I'll tell you........ I was in Viet Nam twice during the 60's and if you think that wasn't a "shooting" war then you might take a good look at some documentaries on the Viet Nam war.... I lost countless friends needlessly -- In the last 56 years we have had Korea, Viet Nam, and the Gulf wars - <P>I'm not flaming you here but to me, anytime lives are lost it is a "shooting" war. I was one of the fortunate ones who got to come home alive. There is not a day that goes by that I don't pray for the others. And, as for the initiative of today - I pray for those men and women too!<P>You simply do not know what war is unless you have lived it.......||||reg||1
Z||000017||2birds||11-11-2001||09:13 PM||||tgowens,<P>No flame taken, and I hope you feel the same way.<P>Please understand that I did not mean to take anything away from the people who fought and died or lost friends/loved ones in any of the wars/conflicts (or whatever the powers that be call them).  Our countries asked, and they answered the call, plain and simple.  A loss is a loss, be it one person or millions, and they all deserve the same gratitude and respect equally.  I'm fortunate enough to be fairly isolated and relatively safe in my chosen military profession (submarines), and I have nothing but the utmost respect and admiration for those who put their lives on the line in a much more obvious fashion.  Modern sub duty pales in comparison with ground troops and aircrew, hazard-wise.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||blazin'||11-11-2001||09:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BC-GT:<BR><B> That's truely disgusting, I don't generally get pissed at much, but I'd probably start swinging at those f*ckers for something like that. Anyone here think they got a hard gig? try a couple of days in the trenches like they did in ww1. This is the ONE holiday I got lots of respect for.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Considering that these guys risked their lives for our freedom, we'd think that even the lowest of ppl would show some respect!!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||loafer87gt||11-11-2001||11:36 PM||||I find that the remembrance day ceremonies I attend on Nov 11th seem to becoming more and more like attending a church service. I was raised in a small town of about 800 people, and I go there each year to pay my respects to my grandfather and the men he fought alongside with. Each year the number of living veterans seems to get lower and lower, and instead of them leading the ceremony it is now hosted by the different pastors and ministers of our local churches. It just doesn't seem to have the same impact when you have a priest who has never been involved in any sort of conflict talk about the past wars, as opposed to hearing the personal tales of the veterans themselves. I also found it to be in very poor taste when one of the pastors talked for about 15 minutes on how war is not the answer, and that if we loved our neighbours that war would be not be necessary. He related this to the present Afghani/West situation and stated how if we loved our neighbours more, and shared our prosperity with them the Sept. 11th act or other wars would not have happened. I found his statements to be very disrespectful to the veterans present, as he basically said that their fighting was pointless. Methinks he needs to pull his head out of his ass and take off his rose colored glasses. ||||reg||1
